---
code: apply-plan-not-dispatchable
envelope: invocation
when-fires: a `dorc apply --host` invocation could not turn the bytes it was handed into something a dispatch permit may be minted over, so it shipped nothing. cli/main.rs apply_refused and apply_edge_refused, over cli/apply.rs consented_apply's own refusal and over the local durable edge that would not open. {reason} is the closed word for which step did not close: `image-not-recordable` (the bytes are past what one exact image may carry), `session-not-preparable` (the session could not bind its own assignment), a `receipt-not-*` word (the document the store would not take), or a root/key/store word straight off the local edge (`no-controller-root`, `store-not-a-directory`, a keyset state, ...). {store} is the per-user state base this apply would have filed under. The edge words are reachable from the shipped binary and covered at cli/tests/durable_route.rs; the first two are not, which is why this case drives the harness defect route.
why: 30Rb apply-dispatch-sequence - an intent binds EXACT bytes, so bytes that cannot be recorded exactly are bytes no permit may be minted over and no dispatch may follow. One code carrying a reason WORD rather than sibling codes (28L rul-reason-enums-not-sibling-codes): the world is one - an apply that bound nothing and shipped nothing - and only the step that did not close differs. The word is the most specific one each route has, because rounding them all to one `intent-not-published` named the step everyone could already see and dropped the only thing a reader acts on (30Rs fix-apply-durable-reporting); the store rides beside it because the same word means different repairs at a per-user root and at a named one. Remediation register (help) wanted: per reason, and for the image bound that means naming the bound rather than the file.
---
